[{:db/id "q-351648/field-1/d973c438d56c14d02356cf80526d8cf5fc716ed8ea2e5ca225983e202aba0bce" :answer/id #uuid "11144a52-1fcd-5925-9f11-bb2e344adf0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351648/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8e0e928a-bcf6-506a-b094-a4dbd8ee2560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351648/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e51c125d-1370-5f22-88b9-a7c316a2ec1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351648/field-1/d973c438d56c14d02356cf80526d8cf5fc716ed8ea2e5ca225983e202aba0bce" :answer-field/choices ["q-351648/field-1/d973c438d56c14d02356cf80526d8cf5fc716ed8ea2e5ca225983e202aba0bce" "q-351648/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351648" :question/id #uuid "131e703a-a903-5d2c-974d-c9d434f7a7ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-351648" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351648/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection/671a536cb9a5f381de66ea91d9bc1b45b9fb8a1622f157d27c3c01d9a2897119" :answer/id #uuid "58d590df-d578-586d-b487-681580fc7c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{e}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection/16ce5c05cae5537ff34d35f2a67f9a5ca756cf735ea3a33c52cee599025c970d" :answer/id #uuid "4696b8c0-1462-5419-864c-23dad534836f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection/afc20dfd83d9ebdc9583611df6359cf13b11b070a503304768180d9723979995" :answer/id #uuid "4180c806-93a6-5c74-9ab9-e03255d2aee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection/ec80dd5e3949a7adc8ec279b0396106c6bf1d5ee1f3bdacaa6d6fd13d76c4b13" :answer/id #uuid "99ef4a93-c22e-57ef-9fa6-9897b2c3cbc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{e}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection/acf7aaf79f83e0a1ab6787fe0148237d2f04aefa585b6cf8f8e2448a74ae60ee" :answer/id #uuid "d7365e2d-ab74-5f32-a0cd-74652713e8f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1ee313-410f-50a6-99f9-5353e66fb635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120841/selection/afc20dfd83d9ebdc9583611df6359cf13b11b070a503304768180d9723979995" :answer-field/choices ["q-120841/selection/671a536cb9a5f381de66ea91d9bc1b45b9fb8a1622f157d27c3c01d9a2897119" "q-120841/selection/16ce5c05cae5537ff34d35f2a67f9a5ca756cf735ea3a33c52cee599025c970d" "q-120841/selection/afc20dfd83d9ebdc9583611df6359cf13b11b070a503304768180d9723979995" "q-120841/selection/ec80dd5e3949a7adc8ec279b0396106c6bf1d5ee1f3bdacaa6d6fd13d76c4b13" "q-120841/selection/acf7aaf79f83e0a1ab6787fe0148237d2f04aefa585b6cf8f8e2448a74ae60ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120841" :question/id #uuid "2e5a3e91-0e54-58ef-b48b-8adb00370d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120841" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}$ | $\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120841/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120841"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-348069/field-1/07649648f9a9389bf8e135af2d7aedf473ecd34eae827584221227da3722a732" :answer/id #uuid "74690141-04cc-5a4e-b855-0adfdbc1b61b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348069/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c136aa62-560a-5f39-8d16-89d554dc8dbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348069/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "70c1ae38-5640-5e62-b35b-9835cec1cf71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348069/field-1/07649648f9a9389bf8e135af2d7aedf473ecd34eae827584221227da3722a732" :answer-field/choices ["q-348069/field-1/07649648f9a9389bf8e135af2d7aedf473ecd34eae827584221227da3722a732" "q-348069/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348069" :question/id #uuid "54be50ac-2279-5ba6-a1f2-5576a1ca045e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348069" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-348069/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348069"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351669/field-1/b534fc82a381e66f3167ba57cb5e7d08d88e9dce97f3edf10738b1c11b15a695" :answer/id #uuid "2c09665f-eeed-54bf-9cf6-aa26d3b7b748" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351669/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cda0fe82-85d9-5c48-94d1-8a648ddbc9d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351669/field-1/b534fc82a381e66f3167ba57cb5e7d08d88e9dce97f3edf10738b1c11b15a695" :answer-field/choices ["q-351669/field-1/b534fc82a381e66f3167ba57cb5e7d08d88e9dce97f3edf10738b1c11b15a695"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351669" :question/id #uuid "17420bc3-cd32-5b9b-bcee-d0c6c81b8d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-351669" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3\\sqrt{xy},\\,y(4)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3\\sqrt{xy} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $9\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351669/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351652/field-1/e952dafebd261786cbd292d21a3aad20cc73037737a0c91db92ba3b168c3bd97" :answer/id #uuid "2471c42e-6c8e-5d5b-bdbb-3e6e8633092f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\ln(e+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351652/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5da857-1a56-548a-adb9-3772e71b44fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351652/field-1/e952dafebd261786cbd292d21a3aad20cc73037737a0c91db92ba3b168c3bd97" :answer-field/choices ["q-351652/field-1/e952dafebd261786cbd292d21a3aad20cc73037737a0c91db92ba3b168c3bd97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351652" :question/id #uuid "3a7012b4-ba96-5229-94f8-65992f0418c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-351652" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y),\\,y(1)=\\frac{e}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)$ | $\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351652/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "aef65cad-d63f-54c7-a8bb-ff607649a482" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8a04fe22-7bce-5c04-83cd-6db81a06efeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "bf359880-d622-5d70-b737-46ad25e750ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "4ed7a731-88f6-569d-b478-e86beb443a1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "419f924f-329e-5714-81c4-9b6c6fc8767a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c10be299-5874-50d8-843c-b28c4828d19c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-53858/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-53858/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-53858/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-53858/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-53858/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-53858/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-53858" :question/id #uuid "61c1b09e-425b-5c97-b1c1-c9772c7f5a2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-53858" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-53858/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-53858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351664/field-1/d21e25585001c6149b5b6527de96daf54475d57bf270001e88236fe510721fc8" :answer/id #uuid "ce43bd4c-a5c3-5581-ae55-b0886b540098" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{41}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351664/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "83eeb7d2-77a7-5fb4-841e-cff41828c729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351664/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0342e1a4-4cb2-587e-bcee-1083b0524bec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351664/field-1/d21e25585001c6149b5b6527de96daf54475d57bf270001e88236fe510721fc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-351664/field-1/d21e25585001c6149b5b6527de96daf54475d57bf270001e88236fe510721fc8" "q-351664/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351664" :question/id #uuid "9934ea59-aac3-571d-b640-c47558c283b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351664" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+2,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is $\\frac{3}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot 0+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{41}{8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351664/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "2eeb0c0a-0f18-5722-9d42-cc439deb76bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "045d835a-a24c-5a67-b57a-d6af180f0cf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a548520b-477b-5f09-bb35-a771046631a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "615b070b-8f92-51f3-b1db-584c0b8d8de1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9d16466a-78f2-5e8d-a30a-a19d56d9d967" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "51484944-46b4-5c49-9e9e-22f3d0816ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117949/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-117949/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-117949/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-117949/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-117949/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-117949/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117949" :question/id #uuid "e42fdbba-ebdf-5819-9169-b749d4c3dbaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-117949" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={1}^{2}-{2}^{2}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117949/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117949"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "97ab9d36-d352-528d-a429-e579ef29d643" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "35169e20-348f-576b-b141-30977b4990c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "1cbeb325-1b50-5c33-b27d-0ad9f19b5b35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "9317035e-f1e9-5659-8658-390032c3bf94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "5e3804a2-5cf9-5706-bbd4-68f131c4c98e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "74781dd8-fa34-54e3-ba7a-74dfaca2c650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117942/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-117942/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-117942/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-117942/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-117942/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-117942/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117942" :question/id #uuid "188a5b3c-5f60-5e50-a9a9-8f590c5807a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117942" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 1}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117942/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117942"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351665/field-1/84ba06c5ef46c5ec9648beb7e3447fc668d456369383b0c063fb4b9a83b2adb9" :answer/id #uuid "eba9e838-7ade-5883-9d60-e8d2c434c607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351665/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa21a63-351b-5d7a-9f78-36c54fc9d850" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351665/field-1/84ba06c5ef46c5ec9648beb7e3447fc668d456369383b0c063fb4b9a83b2adb9" :answer-field/choices ["q-351665/field-1/84ba06c5ef46c5ec9648beb7e3447fc668d456369383b0c063fb4b9a83b2adb9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351665" :question/id #uuid "56adedde-b81f-5ddc-bdf9-81a9bca9e343" :question/math-academy-id "q-351665" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x+4,\\,y(1)=0.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot 1+4=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =0+Δy=0+12=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+21=33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $14$ | $21$ |\n| $4$ | $33$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈33.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351665/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351665"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ab2ac0fc-79e1-5a62-83b0-2ca5f3f04743" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "6ac3239d-0149-501a-a0d5-f27013991428" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "1dbf82a0-a6b6-5e36-a5e9-4ad812af04c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1d6f6249-c6f4-5d6e-a38a-3cd70ea0f22c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "29ee4c43-6b3f-53fa-97d1-2a7c08a40bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f295d12-946c-54f9-a31f-23e3520f8928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117968/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-117968/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-117968/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-117968/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-117968/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-117968/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117968" :question/id #uuid "ae67b5f0-7d38-5916-8b7c-e399d74aab69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117968" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117968/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117968"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351673/field-1/911929c1b4f2d7b940a755171a432f2ecc740a9d24ccb5e949a3e2a03d23708f" :answer/id #uuid "93949281-e59f-55e7-9a19-f44389ee7540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+e+e^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351673/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9df77caf-9766-52db-b28d-6d7a8c0d3869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351673/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac1b7429-79d0-5eb1-97e5-adca3823afa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351673/field-1/911929c1b4f2d7b940a755171a432f2ecc740a9d24ccb5e949a3e2a03d23708f" :answer-field/choices ["q-351673/field-1/911929c1b4f2d7b940a755171a432f2ecc740a9d24ccb5e949a3e2a03d23708f" "q-351673/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351673" :question/id #uuid "c99ae56b-4149-54c1-8619-9d86d9094795" :question/math-academy-id "q-351673" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{0}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{1}=e \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=e\\cdot 1=e \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx={e}^{2}\\cdot 1={e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=(1+e)+{e}^{2}=1+e+{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $1+e+{e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1+e+{e}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351673/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351673"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "d6f0a1d4-1656-5411-81bb-7117d300d326" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "d43e3ac0-1047-57bf-a430-c847c57e8601" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "3d6c6459-362e-5fbf-927a-666de1be3a86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "47da7e62-22ed-57c7-b0d8-08737aa30cec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e63f72d6-3cca-5064-bde2-f203dc1f14fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ddb6dcc-ad58-5951-ae57-ffbdd29082b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120909/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-120909/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" "q-120909/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-120909/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-120909/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-120909/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120909" :question/id #uuid "aeb94dfd-5a1b-5bab-9680-a3138483317b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120909" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-x,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=2-1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=3-2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+1=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=4-3=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120909/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120909"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "52779d14-462d-5b4e-ad25-4431e978b600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "d37cc236-7228-5590-bab2-b7109ee5088e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "07921772-6b04-58ee-b01b-10fed701cbe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "275c7f87-7071-557a-a02e-630e8284c218" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "f26c1616-c6ad-5d2a-a546-bbef947d04af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "18c356de-a317-57fb-90eb-abb0739d19f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120940/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-120940/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" "q-120940/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" "q-120940/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-120940/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-120940/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120940" :question/id #uuid "f8822200-e36c-5c35-9d79-59d553b362a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120940" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120940/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120940"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-348078/field-1/a0aacfad5e63b630aadfa37f02ad0f2efcc76e3e3f20b63742550fcd9366dadd" :answer/id #uuid "f8008618-6302-5618-9126-d3830a38f3cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "431" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348078/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c637b5e0-9f65-5011-882c-28128a54b585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348078/field-1/a0aacfad5e63b630aadfa37f02ad0f2efcc76e3e3f20b63742550fcd9366dadd" :answer-field/choices ["q-348078/field-1/a0aacfad5e63b630aadfa37f02ad0f2efcc76e3e3f20b63742550fcd9366dadd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348078" :question/id #uuid "743b5bad-b7a5-5a83-963e-6ab7935e51a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-348078" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(6).$ \n\n$y(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(6).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=6$ is $6,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-0}{3}=\\frac{6}{3}=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={0}^{2}+3(1)=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 2=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={2}^{2}+3(7)=25 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=25\\cdot 2=50 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+2=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=7+50=57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={4}^{2}+3(57)=187 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=187\\cdot 2=374 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+2=6 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=57+374=431\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $187$ | $374$ |\n| $6$ | $431$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(6)≈431.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-348078/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348078"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7701"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-537"] :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9354"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
